1
00:00:00,000 --> 00:00:09,517
One failed road, bridge, or rail link can change how an entire city moves, even far from
the damaged link.

2
00:00:09,517 --> 00:00:14,000
The impact does not stop at the disruption.

3
00:00:14,000 --> 00:00:22,080
Traffic shifts to other corridors. Congestion grows, journeys take longer, and essential
services become harder to reach.

4
00:00:22,080 --> 00:00:31,000
A local failure can create a much wider problem for communities. Those effects are often
hidden by citywide averages.

5
00:00:31,000 --> 00:00:39,808
I built ResiliRoute to make these cascading effects visible, and help people explore
transportation resilience before a disruption happens.

6
00:00:39,808 --> 00:00:44,000
It is a tool for understanding network vulnerability.

7
00:00:44,000 --> 00:00:50,997
Here is Harbor City: a synthetic demonstration network with twenty-four nodes and forty-
two transport links.

8
00:00:50,997 --> 00:00:56,920
Residential areas generate travel demand. Stations, hospitals, shelters, and commercial
districts provide destinations.

9
00:00:56,920 --> 00:01:03,262
Under normal conditions, the model calculates an average journey of fourteen point two
minutes.

10
00:01:03,262 --> 00:01:09,000
Each journey contributes demand to the links it uses, creating network-wide volumes.

11
00:01:09,000 --> 00:01:16,540
Now I run the earthquake demo. Harbor Bridge, a rail connector, and a coastal road
become unavailable.

12
00:01:16,540 --> 00:01:23,525
Routes are recalculated, and travel demand is reassigned to the remaining links. The
results change immediately.

13
00:01:23,525 --> 00:01:32,608
Average travel time rises to twenty point nine minutes. Essential access falls from
ninety-four point four to seventy-eight point nine percent.

14
00:01:32,608 --> 00:01:36,236
The map also shows which links are closed.

15
00:01:36,236 --> 00:01:40,000
These are calculated outputs, not preset disaster scores.

16
00:01:40,000 --> 00:01:50,154
The affected traffic does not simply disappear. On Port Bypass, demand increases from
about four hundred to over three thousand trips per hour.

17
00:01:50,154 --> 00:01:57,622
This alternative corridor becomes overloaded. Comparing routes shows how the same
hospital journey becomes a longer detour.

18
00:01:57,622 --> 00:02:04,000
Orange corridors highlight demand at or above capacity, revealing bottlenecks beyond the
original closure.

19
00:02:04,000 --> 00:02:11,628
The dashboard compares travel time, congestion, accessibility, network efficiency, and
resilience before and after the disruption.

20
00:02:11,628 --> 00:02:20,211
Resilience falls from a baseline of one hundred to sixty-nine. Every indicator has a
published definition in the methodology.

21
00:02:20,211 --> 00:02:27,000
Accessibility measures population-weighted access to hospitals, shelters, and stations
within their specified travel-time thresholds.

22
00:02:27,000 --> 00:02:36,307
The stress test removes each available link individually, reruns the simulation, and
ranks its additional impact on the current scenario.

23
00:02:36,307 --> 00:02:44,889
Harbor Rail is now the most critical remaining link. Separately, restoring Harbor Bridge
offers the largest modeled recovery benefit.

24
00:02:44,889 --> 00:02:50,000
The ranking measures modeled consequences, not the probability of failure.

25
00:02:50,000 --> 00:02:59,965
The East district is hit hardest. Its average travel time almost doubles, while
essential access drops from one hundred to sixty-seven percent.

26
00:02:59,965 --> 00:03:05,909
The hospital analysis reveals which communities lose access within the model's travel-
time threshold.

27
00:03:05,909 --> 00:03:10,000
Mobility resilience is also an issue of equity.

28
00:03:10,000 --> 00:03:19,307
The resilience analyst turns structured simulation evidence into a concise brief: what
changed, why it matters, and possible planning actions.

29
00:03:19,307 --> 00:03:27,889
This demo uses the deterministic local analyst. An optional server-side language model
uses the same evidence, with automatic fallback.

30
00:03:27,889 --> 00:03:33,000
The language model explains results; it does not calculate them.

31
00:03:33,000 --> 00:03:38,580
Graph routing, demand assignment, congestion modeling, and accessibility analysis drive
the results.

32
00:03:38,580 --> 00:03:45,000
All computations use transparent, documented rules. This educational prototype supports
understanding, not emergency navigation.

33
00:03:45,000 --> 00:03:50,000
ResiliRoute. Helping communities understand disruption before it happens.
